From: kterm
Subject: Redraw only the rows written to, not the whole terminal

The rows written to while processing output are gathered in a bounding box
that starts empty and grows with each character. It started the wrong way
round, top at -G_MAXINT and bottom at G_MAXINT: every row on screen, so
that any output, a single character typed, redrew the whole terminal.
post_CMD() restarts it the right way round, empty.

On an e-ink screen every redraw is a refresh of the area drawn: the whole
terminal for each character, slower, using more power, and leaving more
behind than the row that changed.

--- a/src/vte.cc
+++ b/src/vte.cc
@@ -136,8 +136,10 @@
 
 class Terminal::ProcessingContext {
 public:
-        vte::grid::row_t m_bbox_top{-G_MAXINT};
-        vte::grid::row_t m_bbox_bottom{G_MAXINT};
+        // empty: grown by each character written (see pre_GRAPHIC() and
+        // post_GRAPHIC()), as when restarted in post_CMD()
+        vte::grid::row_t m_bbox_top{G_MAXINT};
+        vte::grid::row_t m_bbox_bottom{-G_MAXINT};
         bool m_modified{false};
         bool m_bottom{false};
         bool m_invalidated_text{false};
